    <section class="pbs-sec" id="pbs-faq">
      <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

      <details class="pbs-faq"><summary>What is the exact formula for a pear body shape?</summary><div class="pbs-faq-body">
        <p>Hips at least 3.6 inches wider than the bust, and a hip-to-waist gap of under 9 inches. Both parts matter — the first rules out rectangle and hourglass shapes, and the second is what separates pear from bottom hourglass, which has the same wider-hip pattern but a much more defined waist.</p>
      </div></details>

      <details class="pbs-faq"><summary>Is pear the same as spoon body shape?</summary><div class="pbs-faq-body">
        <p>They're related but not identical. Spoon is a more specific pattern where there's a pronounced "shelf" at the high hip, just below the waist, measured separately from the full hip. Pear is the broader category. If a calculator gives you spoon specifically, pear styling advice still applies — the shelf mainly affects fit at the high-hip area of trousers and skirts.</p>
      </div></details>

      <details class="pbs-faq"><summary>Pear vs bottom hourglass — which one am I?</summary><div class="pbs-faq-body">
        <p>Check the gap between your hips and your waist. Under 9 inches, you're a pear. Nine inches or more, you're a bottom hourglass. Both have hips wider than the bust — the waist definition is the entire difference.</p>
      </div></details>

      <details class="pbs-faq"><summary>Is a pear shape healthier than an apple shape?</summary><div class="pbs-faq-body">
        <p>In terms of fat-distribution risk specifically, research generally does favour pear over apple — hip and thigh fat behaves differently, metabolically, than abdominal fat, and is linked to lower cardiovascular risk at a comparable body size. That's a population-level pattern, not an individual guarantee; overall health depends on far more than shape alone.</p>
      </div></details>

      <details class="pbs-faq"><summary>Can a pear shape change with weight loss?</summary><div class="pbs-faq-body">
        <p>Weight loss reduces fat everywhere to some degree, but it tends to make an existing shape more pronounced rather than converting it into a different one — hip and thigh fat is often the last to reduce for pear-shaped bodies specifically. Strength training that builds the shoulders and upper back is a more direct way to shift the visual proportion.</p>
      </div></details>

      <details class="pbs-faq"><summary>What's the most flattering swimsuit for a pear shape?</summary><div class="pbs-faq-body">
        <p>Tops with detail, structure or a high neckline paired with a plain, darker bottom in boyshort or high-waisted styles. The goal is the same as with everyday clothing: add visual interest above the waist so the frame reads as balanced rather than bottom-heavy.</p>
      </div></details>
    </section>

    <p>Most "pear body shape" advice online skips the waist-to-hip distinction entirely and treats every wider-hipped body the same way — which is how pear and bottom hourglass end up with identical styling tips despite needing slightly different approaches. Once you know which side of that 9-inch line you're on, the advice gets a lot more specific.</p>

